---
/**
 * Book I, Ch. X, Prop. 3: a GRU in PyTorch's convention. One state line; the
 * update gate keeps a share z of it and writes 1 − z of the candidate.
 */
---
<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 720 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="gu-t gu-d" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" style="--fig-min: 640px">
  <title id="gu-t">A GRU cell and its state line</title>
  <desc id="gu-d">
    A horizontal line carries the hidden state from left to right. It is
    multiplied by the update gate z and then receives the candidate scaled by
    one minus z. The reset gate feeds the candidate, deciding how much of the
    preceding state it reads. Both gates and the candidate read the preceding
    hidden state and the current input.
  </desc>

  <text x="0" y="22" class="t-lab">the state line</text>

  <line x1="20" y1="70" x2="700" y2="70" class="s3" stroke-width="2" />
  <text x="20" y="58" class="t-key">h(t−1)</text>
  <text x="700" y="58" class="t-key" text-anchor="end">h(t)</text>

  <circle cx="250" cy="70" r="14" class="bx-fill" />
  <text x="250" y="76" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">×</text>
  <circle cx="470" cy="70" r="14" class="bx-fill" />
  <text x="470" y="76" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">+</text>

  <rect x="180" y="200" width="140" height="48" class="bx-fill" />
  <text x="250" y="221" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">update gate z</text>
  <text x="250" y="239" class="t-sm" text-anchor="middle">σ → (0, 1)</text>
  <line x1="250" y1="198" x2="250" y2="96" class="wire" />
  <polygon points="245,96 250,84 255,96" class="head" />
  <text x="258" y="130" class="t-key">z: keep</text>

  <circle cx="470" cy="140" r="14" class="bx-fill" />
  <text x="470" y="146" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">×</text>
  <line x1="470" y1="126" x2="470" y2="96" class="wire" />
  <polygon points="465,96 470,84 475,96" class="head" />
  <line x1="300" y1="198" x2="458" y2="148" class="wire" />
  <text x="322" y="156" class="t-key">1 − z: write</text>

  <rect x="400" y="200" width="140" height="48" class="bx-fill" />
  <text x="470" y="221" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">candidate n</text>
  <text x="470" y="239" class="t-sm" text-anchor="middle">tanh → (−1, 1)</text>
  <line x1="470" y1="198" x2="470" y2="154" class="wire" />

  <rect x="580" y="200" width="120" height="48" class="bx-fill" />
  <text x="640" y="221" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">reset gate r</text>
  <text x="640" y="239" class="t-sm" text-anchor="middle">σ → (0, 1)</text>
  <line x1="578" y1="224" x2="552" y2="224" class="wire" />
  <polygon points="552,219 540,224 552,229" class="head" />

  <line x1="20" y1="280" x2="700" y2="280" class="wire-soft" />
  {[250, 470, 640].map((cx) => (
    <line x1={cx} y1="280" x2={cx} y2="250" class="wire-soft" />
  ))}
  <text x="20" y="298" class="t-sm">h(t−1), x(t): both gates and the candidate read the same two vectors</text>

  <text x="0" y="324" class="t-sm">Holding the gates fixed, the state line's derivative is diag(z). Other papers swap z and 1 − z.</text>
</svg>
